On 10/30/06, Yanis Batura <ybatura@mail.ru> wrote:
>
> {galfi} meant changing the *content* of the article, and indeed,
> {ba lo nu galfi} the former contents change into something new.
> {stika} seems weaker for me here, since it supposes (slight)
> adjustments of what is already present in general, whereas a
> Wikipedia article can be hugely changed in one edit.

Yes, it is possible to replace an article with a completely
different one, but that's not what "edit" means. The general
idea of Wikipedia is that articles can be continually edited,
i.e. corrected, modified and added to. Even though {galfi} is
a correct decription of what sometimes happens when one
edits an article, it is not the typical thing that happens.

